                               Review by liggitt                                

Summary                                                                         

3 comments to consider below.                                                   

Inline Comments                                                                 

pkg/controller/podautoscaler/metrics/utilization.go (confidence: 0.80)          

▌ case percentage.Cmp(big.NewInt(math.MinInt32)) < 0: currentUtilization =    
▌ math.MinInt32                                                               

can metric.Value or request actually be negative by the time they get here? if  
not, this branch is unreachable and I'd rather not carry a rail nothing can     
hit... if they can, none of the four new table rows exercise it, so add one that
lands on math.MinInt32 and checks rawAverageValue stays negative as well        

pkg/controller/podautoscaler/metrics/utilization.go (confidence: 0.60)          

▌ return float64(currentUtilization) / float64(targetUtilization),            
▌ currentUtilization, rawAverageValue, nil                                    

utilizationRatio is a float64, so it doesn't need to inherit the int32 clamp... 
the old code derived it from the already-truncated currentUtilization too, but  
that was a bug, not a contract. maybe compute the ratio from percentage before  
narrowing (a big.Float quotient, or percentage.Int64() guarded by an IsInt64()  
check) so the reported percentage saturates but the scaling decision uses the   
real value?                                                                     

pkg/controller/podautoscaler/metrics/utilization_test.go (confidence: 0.55)     

▌                                                                             
▌      name: "utilization percentage exceeds int32",                          
▌      metrics: PodMetricsInfo{                                               
▌          "test-pod-0": {Value: 1 << 40},                                    
▌                                                                             

the four new rows all sit exactly on the overflow boundary, which is good...    
also add one row that sits just under math.MaxInt32 so we know the clamp isn't  
kicking in early (e.g. Value: math.MaxInt32 / 100 * 1, request: 1, expecting the
exact percentage back)                                                          
